  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1239 PM CDT THU SEP 10 2026\n\nA very welcomed, but rather brief pattern shift is expected\nthrough the weekend, as upper-level ridging breaks down ahead of\nan approaching trough and cold front. As of this morning, it\nstill appears as if the cold front will become disconnected from\nthe main upper-level low, eventually stalling somewhere to our\nnorth. There has been a change in this regard, as it now appears\nif the cold front will stall farther south, providing us with\nbetter forcing. Paired with stout southerly flow, and a weak\ndisturbance riding a small trough, rain chances should increase\ndramatically as we head into Friday. Since we've been so dry, no\nflooding or flash flooding is anticipated, but rainfall rates\ncould be moderate to heavy at times with the strongest afternoon\nactivity.\n\nThe bulk of the rainfall activity will occur through Saturday,\nand the upper-level ridge will begin to build back in by Sunday.\nThere will be some lingering rain chances during the afternoon\nhours, but those will be more scattered in nature. By the start\nof the new workweek, the ridge will build once more, with high\nconfidence in afternoon highs returning to the low and mid-90s on\nMonday. Here is where afternoon highs will remain for most of\nnext week, with scattered thunderstorms chances in the afternoon\nassociated with this typical \"summertime\" pattern.\n\n/44/",
